WEBVTT

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:38.140
<v Ira Glass>My grandfather, Melvin, ran a hardware store in Baltimore that also sold model trains. And he had a huge-- and I mean huge-- train set in the basement of his house on Pimlico Road that I loved. This thing was vast, with tunnels and bridges and towns and streetlights that lit. And the train engines puff smoke as they scooted around the track on this big, wooden platform in the basement in the middle of all this epic amount of clutter and junk and his tools and, for some reason, a mimeograph machine.
<v Ira Glass>我祖父梅尔文（Melvin）在巴尔的摩经营一家五金店，同时也卖火车模型。他在皮姆利科路（Pimlico Road）家中的地下室里有一个巨大的——我是说真的巨大——火车模型，我非常喜欢。这套模型非常广阔，有隧道、桥梁、城镇和会发光的街灯。火车头在地下室那块巨大的木制平台上绕着轨道行驶时还会喷出烟雾，而周围堆满了各种极其杂乱的破烂、他的工具，以及不知为何放在那儿的一台油印机。

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:58.570
<v Ira Glass>And in the corner of the basement was the filthiest toilet I had ever seen-- like, frighteningly dirty to me as a little suburban kid. The whole basement was a chamber of chaos, and it was the most unsettlingly-chaotic thing in it. But that is not the story I'm here to tell. The story I'm here to tell is about Melvin and one of his sisters.
<v Ira Glass>地下室的角落里还有我见过最脏的厕所——作为一个郊区长大的小孩，我觉得它脏得吓人。整个地下室就像一个混乱的巢穴，而那个厕所是其中最让人不安、最混乱的东西。但这并不是我要讲的故事。我要讲的是关于梅尔文和他其中一个姐妹的故事。

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.960
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGS]
<v Ira Glass>[电话铃声]

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.860
<v Uncle Bennett>Hello?
<v Uncle Bennett>喂？

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:02.940
<v Ira Glass>Uncle Bennett?
<v Ira Glass>本内特（Bennett）叔叔？

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.550
<v Uncle Bennett>Yes, you got it.
<v Uncle Bennett>没错，是我。

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:12.240
<v Ira Glass>To be sure I was getting the facts absolutely right on this thing, I called the last person in the family who might remember it, my Uncle Bennett. He was at home.
<v Ira Glass>为了确保我了解的事实绝对准确，我打电话给了家里唯一可能还记得这件事的人，我的本内特叔叔。他当时在家。

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.510
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGS]
<v Ira Glass>[电话铃声]

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:14.550
<v Uncle Bennett>Wait a minute. Hold on for one second.
<v Uncle Bennett>等一下。稍微等一下。

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.500
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, well do.
<v Ira Glass>好的，没问题。

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:24.030
<v Uncle Bennett>Yeah, yeah. Hello? Hello? Yeah, Renee?
<v Uncle Bennett>好了，好了。喂？喂？哦，蕾妮（Renee）吗？

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.670
<v Ira Glass>Renee is Bennett's girlfriend. They live together.
<v Ira Glass>蕾妮是本内特的女朋友。他们住在一起。

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.940
<v Uncle Bennett>That was Renee. She wasn't sure if I was home.
<v Uncle Bennett>刚才那是蕾妮。她不确定我是不是在家。

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.410
<v Ira Glass>Ah.
<v Ira Glass>啊。

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:31.500
<v Uncle Bennett>All right, anyway--
<v Uncle Bennett>好吧，总之——

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.440
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. Is she in the house and she called you?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。她在屋里，然后她打电话给你？

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.620
<v Uncle Bennett>Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.
<v Uncle Bennett>对。对。对。

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:50.490
<v Ira Glass>I guess you're getting this-- these are older people who do not want to be bothered to cross the house or yell from room to room. Anyway, the story-- the story that I'm here to tell about my grandfather, Melvin, and his brothers and sisters.
<v Ira Glass>我想你大概明白了——这些上了年纪的人不想费劲走过整个屋子，也不想隔着房间大喊大叫。总之，故事——我要讲关于我祖父梅尔文和他兄弟姐妹的故事。

00:01:50.490 --> 00:02:07.320
<v Ira Glass>Let me just give you the family geography here. He's one of four kids. And for the purposes of this particular story, you should imagine them in two competing teams. There are the two older kids, Melvin and Dorothy, and the two younger kids, Ray and Calvin.
<v Ira Glass>让我先给你梳理一下家庭成员结构。他是四个孩子中的一个。为了讲好这个故事，你应该把他们想象成两支竞争的队伍。年纪大的两个是梅尔文和多萝西（Dorothy），年纪小的两个是雷（Ray）和卡尔文（Calvin）。

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:20.020
<v Ira Glass>OK, Melvin and Dorothy, Ray and Calvin. It's 1964, and their mom dies. The four kids are all in their 40s and 50s at this point. And then just after she died--
<v Ira Glass>好的，梅尔文和多萝西，雷和卡尔文。那是1964年，他们的母亲去世了。当时这四个孩子都已经四五十岁了。就在她去世后——

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:42.490
<v Uncle Bennett>What happened was, Ray came up from Florida. And Calvin and Ray started taking things out of the house-- the silver tea service I remember that she had in the dining room. Whatever she had of value, they took.
<v Uncle Bennett>发生的事情是，雷从佛罗里达州回来了。接着卡尔文和雷开始把家里的东西往外搬——我记得她餐厅里有套银质茶具。凡是值钱的东西，他们全拿走了。

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:47.110
<v Ira Glass>And there was china, right? Like, the thing that I had always heard is that there was china and silverware.
<v Ira Glass>还有瓷器，对吧？我一直听说的就是有瓷器和银器。

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:59.920
<v Uncle Bennett>Yeah, china, silverware, things like that. They just came in like hordes of locusts. And my father got nothing. And Dorothy apparently allegedly got nothing.
<v Uncle Bennett>对，瓷器、银器之类的。他们简直像蝗虫大军一样冲进来。我父亲什么都没得到。多萝西显然据说也没得到任何东西。

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:07.480
<v Ira Glass>Again, the two older kids-- Melvin and Dorothy. The younger kids, Calvin and Ray, took all the good stuff.
<v Ira Glass>再次强调，两个年长的孩子——梅尔文和多萝西。两个小的，卡尔文和雷，拿走了所有好东西。

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.760
<v Uncle Bennett>And then my father was really upset about it.
<v Uncle Bennett>然后我父亲对此非常难过。

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.910
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. And Dorothy too, right?
<v Ira Glass>没错。还有多萝西，对吧？

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.260
<v Uncle Bennett>Yeah, mm-hmm.
<v Uncle Bennett>对，嗯。

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.310
<v Ira Glass>This, Bennett explained to me, was apparently typical behavior for Ray.
<v Ira Glass>本内特向我解释说，这在雷身上是很典型的行为。

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:27.810
<v Uncle Bennett>Ray was always-- like, when she was growing up, they used to say that at dinner, she would take the biggest piece of meat.
<v Uncle Bennett>雷一直都是这样——就像她小时候，大家都说在吃晚饭时，她会抢最大块的肉。

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:38.400
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, yeah. And then the way I remember it is that she takes the stuff, they fight about it, and they never spoke to Ray again.
<v Ira Glass>对，没错。然后我记得的是，她拿走了东西，他们为此吵了一架，从此再也没和雷说过话。

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.800
<v Uncle Bennett>No.
<v Uncle Bennett>没有。

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.890
<v Ira Glass>Didn't talk to her, didn't talk about her.
<v Ira Glass>没再理她，也没再提起她。

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:46.390
<v Uncle Bennett>No, it was just too aggravating, you know?
<v Uncle Bennett>没有，这太让人恼火了，你知道吗？

00:03:46.390 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Ira Glass>Calvin, meanwhile-- Ray's partner in this family heist-- Calvin was a baby in the family, 13 years younger than the next-oldest kid. And the way that I always heard it, nobody held this against Calvin because everybody knew that Ray could just push him around and get him to do whatever she wanted.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，卡尔文——雷在这个家族掠夺案中的同伙——卡尔文是家里的老幺，比前面那个小了13岁。我一直听到的说法是，没人怪罪卡尔文，因为大家都知道雷可以随意指使他，让他做任何她想做的事。

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:10.340
<v Ira Glass>So Calvin was around. I remember Calvin clowning around, doing little magic tricks for me at charity crab feasts that Dorothy used to organize all through my childhood.
<v Ira Glass>所以卡尔文还在家里。我记得卡尔文经常搞怪，在多萝西我童年时期组织的慈善螃蟹宴上，他会为我变些小魔术。

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:26.350
<v Ira Glass>But not Ray. It really was like Ray never existed, was never spoken about, off in Florida. I and my sisters met her only one time. Bennett, her own nephew-- Bennett only met her once on a trip to Miami after he was married.
<v Ira Glass>但雷不是。真的就像雷从未存在过一样，没人提起她，她远在佛罗里达。我和我的姐妹们只见过她一次。本内特，她自己的侄子——本内特也是在他结婚后去迈阿密旅行时才见过她一次。

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:34.690
<v Uncle Bennett>And I called Ray. And she and Uncle Irvin came up, picked us up, and took us to Wolfie's-- [COUGH] pardon me-- which is a deli restaurant.
<v Uncle Bennett>我给雷打了电话。她和欧文（Irvin）叔叔来接我们，带我们去了沃尔菲（Wolfie's）——[咳嗽]抱歉——那是一家熟食餐厅。

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:35.440
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.220
<v Uncle Bennett>That was the only contact I ever had with her my whole life.
<v Uncle Bennett>那是我一生中和她唯一的接触。

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:44.410
<v Ira Glass>And then I remember when Melvin died, Ray not coming to his funeral.
<v Ira Glass>然后我记得梅尔文去世时，雷没有来参加他的葬礼。

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.640
<v Uncle Bennett>No, of course not.
<v Uncle Bennett>不，当然没有。

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:55.300
<v Ira Glass>And I remember being so stunned by that, that a person could be in such a fight with their own sibling that they wouldn't show up at their funeral.
<v Ira Glass>我记得当时非常震惊，一个人竟然能和自己的亲兄弟姐妹闹到这种地步，连葬礼都不出席。

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:09.550
<v Uncle Bennett>Oh, it happens all the time, Ira. And it's usually over money.
<v Uncle Bennett>哦，这种事经常发生，艾拉（Ira）。通常都是为了钱。

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:32.610
<v Ira Glass>I think this story really got to me when I was a kid, and I first heard it because it was the very first glimpse I had at the idea that you can destroy things completely-- like, totally wreck them beyond repair-- with somebody you know so well. I mean, I guess I'm lucky that I lived in a family where something like this would be so rare. But still, for me, personally, that has been a hard lesson to hold on to.
<v Ira Glass>我想这个故事在我小时候第一次听到时就深深触动了我，因为那是我第一次窥见这样一个事实：你可以彻底摧毁一些东西——我是说，彻底毁掉且无法修复——哪怕是和你非常熟悉的人之间。我是说，我想我很幸运能生活在一个这种事很少见的家庭。但即便如此，对我个人而言，这仍然是一个难以接受的惨痛教训。

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:58.470
<v Ira Glass>When I was married and things were going badly and we were in counseling and trying to patch it together for so many years-- like, more years than I want to admit here on the radio-- and I think part of it was because I just had this feeling, like, we are not people who get divorced. Like, we are bigger than that somehow. My mom was a marriage therapist. I just couldn't accept-- I think we both couldn't accept-- that we had just broken things between us so badly that there was no coming back from it.
<v Ira Glass>当我结婚后，情况开始变糟，我们做了很多年的心理咨询试图挽救这段婚姻——比我在广播里愿意承认的时间还要长——我想部分原因是我有一种感觉，觉得我们不是那种会离婚的人。我们某种程度上比这更重要。我母亲是婚姻治疗师。我就是无法接受——我想我们俩都无法接受——我们之间已经把事情搞砸到了无法挽回的地步。

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:28.100
<v Ira Glass>I just didn't want to admit that-- which brings me to Humpty Dumpty. I didn't know this, but some of my coworkers with little kids at home were talking about how unsettling Humpty Dumpty is to some of those kids. Laura said that at the doctor's office, there's a poster. And their two-year-old saw it and after that was like, someone can break and not go back together? And then remembered it when they went back to that office a year later.
<v Ira Glass>我只是不想承认这一点——这让我想到了矮胖子（Humpty Dumpty）。我之前不知道，但几个有小孩的同事聊到矮胖子对一些孩子来说有多么令人不安。劳拉（Laura）说在医生办公室里有一张海报。她两岁的孩子看到了，在那之后就一直在问：一个人破碎了就不能再拼好了吗？一年后他们再去那间办公室时，孩子还记得这件事。

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.100
<v Ira Glass>Zoe's son, Max, is two and a half.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊（Zoe）的儿子马克斯（Max）两岁半。

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:36.790
<v Max>I want to tell him to go back together.
<v Max>我想告诉他把它拼回去。

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.510
<v Zoe>You want it to go back together?
<v Zoe>你想让它复原吗？

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:42.490
<v Max>Yeah, to fix it.
<v Max>对，把它修好。

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.350
<v Zoe>You want to fix Humpty Dumpty?
<v Zoe>你想修好矮胖子？

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:49.330
<v Max>Yeah. I cannot do it.
<v Max>对。我做不到。

00:06:49.330 --> 00:07:13.700
<v Ira Glass>We posted a thing on social media about this and got a ton of responses from parents whose kids were terrified of Humpty Dumpty. One said her preschooler couldn't stand to see her crack eggs in the kitchen because it reminded her of the nursery rhyme. Another said her two-year-old spent weeks asking if he could recreate the story with an egg from the fridge, which they did-- like, a few times-- puzzling over the impossibility of putting an egg back together once it's cracked.
<v Ira Glass>我们在社交媒体上发了关于这个话题的内容，收到了大量家长的反馈，他们的孩子都被矮胖子吓坏了。一位母亲说，她上学前的孩子在厨房看到她敲鸡蛋时都受不了，因为这让她想起了那首童谣。另一位说，她两岁的孩子花了几个星期问能不能用冰箱里的鸡蛋重现这个故事，他们真的试了几次——对着鸡蛋碎了之后无法复原的可能性感到困惑。

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:33.550
<v Ira Glass>A few people wrote to talk about how it messed them up as kids to picture Humpty Dumpty left there in pieces on the side of the road, sad look on his face. A woman named Isabella wrote, "I wondered if he had to exist conscious in his cracked state forever or if he would slowly die. Because he seemed to be still alive after he had cracked."
<v Ira Glass>还有几个人写信说，小时候看着矮胖子被留在路边支离破碎、一脸忧伤的样子，这让他们留下了心理阴影。一位叫伊莎贝拉（Isabella）的女士写道：“我一直在想，他是不是必须永远清醒地感受自己破碎的状态，还是会慢慢死去。因为在他碎了之后，他似乎还活着。”

00:07:33.550 --> 00:08:06.960
<v Ira Glass>There's something about that image of someone broken so badly that nothing can fix it, that things can go so wrong, the finality of that-- before you understand all the bad things that can happen in the world, how things come apart and people you love can die and you will die, before you get to any of the entropic, messy chaos of our world with its blackened toilets and its long-ago trashed model trains, you get this glimpse, this fat, cartoon, overdressed egg man smashed to tiny bits of shell and goo.
<v Ira Glass>那种被严重破坏以至于无法修复的形象有些东西，事情会变得如此糟糕，那种终极的结局——在你理解世界上所有可能发生的坏事之前，在理解事物是如何分崩离析、你所爱的人会去世、你自己也会死之前，在你接触到我们这个世界充满熵增和混乱的泥潭之前——那些发黑的厕所和很久以前报废的火车模型——你先看到了这个瞥见：那个臃肿、卡通、穿着考究的蛋人被砸成了细小的蛋壳和黏液。

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:29.590
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, people who hit a moment when things shatter apart-- now they make sense of it and not go to pieces themselves. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目中，我们将讲述那些在事情分崩离析时经历人生时刻的人们——他们如何去理解这一切，而自己没有随之崩溃。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:52.550
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Toy R Us. Sometimes there's a really specific moment when things fall apart, and you know exactly what the moment was, but you were left with no idea how or why it happened or-- more important-- how to put the thing back together. David Kestenbaum found a family that happened to, a father and son, years ago. And the son has thought about it ever since.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“玩具反斗城”。有时真的有一个特定的时刻，事情彻底瓦解，你知道那个时刻究竟是什么，但你完全不知道它是如何或为何发生的，或者更重要的是——如何把事情恢复原状。大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）发现了一个遇到这种情况的家庭，一对父子，那是多年前的事了。从那以后，儿子就一直记挂着这件事。

00:08:52.550 --> 00:09:00.800
<v David Kestenbaum>The son in this story, his name is Peter Ocko. And I don't usually love hearing people's dreams, but this is how our interview started.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个故事里的儿子，他的名字叫彼得·奥克（Peter Ocko）。我通常不喜欢听别人的梦，但这确实是我们采访的开场。

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.210
<v Peter Ocko>Can I tell you my dream last night?
<v Peter Ocko>我可以讲讲我昨晚做的梦吗？

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:03.530
<v David Kestenbaum>Yes. Oh, yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>当然。哦，好的。

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:31.020
<v Peter Ocko>Because you know I've been sort of worrying about this-- I dreamt that I pulled into a store, ran inside to pick something up. And when I came outside, there was a guy standing there, and he had disassembled my entire car. And the pieces were just all over the parking lot. And I was like what did you do to my car? Why did you take it apart? And he goes, it was broken. We needed to fix it. And then I woke up. And I was like, OK. I'm the car, and you're the guy.
<v Peter Ocko>因为你知道我一直为此感到担忧——我梦见我把车开进了一家店，跑进去买点东西。当我出来时，有一个人站在那儿，他已经把我整辆车拆解了。零件散落在整个停车场。我就问，你对我的车做了什么？你为什么要把它拆了？他说，它坏了。我们需要修好它。然后我就醒了。我当时想，好吧。我就是那辆车，而你就是那个人。

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:41.210
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm happy to be the guy. Let's take the car apart. The whole story starts here. When Peter was a kid, his dad got a kind of dream job. This was in the late '70s.
<v David Kestenbaum>我很乐意当那个人。让我们把车拆开看看。整个故事从这里开始。彼得还是个孩子时，他父亲得到了一份理想的工作。那是在70年代末。

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:47.410
<v Peter Ocko>When I was 11, he got a job at the Milton Bradley Company.
<v Peter Ocko>我11岁的时候，他在米尔顿·布拉德利（Milton Bradley）公司找到了一份工作。

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.550
<v David Kestenbaum>Like the toy company.
<v David Kestenbaum>就是那家玩具公司吗？

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.400
<v Peter Ocko>Yeah, the toy company-- as a toy inventor.
<v Peter Ocko>对，那家玩具公司——担任玩具发明家。

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:52.540
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh my god.
<v David Kestenbaum>天哪。

00:09:52.540 --> 00:10:07.210
<v Peter Ocko>Yeah. Well, that's what I thought too. I was in sixth grade. And this was like, super street cred to go in and say, my dad works at Milton Bradley. Because they were a big deal back then. But they were a big deal for 100 years.
<v Peter Ocko>是啊。嗯，我也是这么想的。我当时上六年级。在当时，能说“我爸爸在米尔顿·布拉德利工作”简直太有面子了。因为他们在那时是大牌公司。他们在那之前已经火了100年了。

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:20.650
<v Peter Ocko>In fact, my dad moved into his cubicle. And he was next to the guy who claimed to have invented Chutes and Ladders. So [LAUGHS] I don't think that's true, but the guy was old enough that it's possible.
<v Peter Ocko>事实上，我父亲搬进了他的隔间。他旁边坐着那个声称发明了“滑梯与梯子”（Chutes and Ladders）的人。所以[笑]我不认为那是真的，但那个人年纪够大，所以也有可能。

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:39.350
<v David Kestenbaum>The job was exciting for Peter's dad too. His dad had always been searching for a way to be successful. And success was the thing he imagined happening all at once. He saw himself as an inventor. And if he just had the right idea, the world would notice. His ship would come in. He actually read a lot of books about ships and captains.
<v David Kestenbaum>这份工作对彼得的父亲来说也很让人兴奋。他父亲一直渴望成功。他把成功想象成突如其来发生的事情。他把自己视为发明家。如果他能想到绝佳的点子，全世界都会注意到他。他总觉得自己会迎来人生的转机。他其实读了很多关于船只和船长的书。

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:49.100
<v David Kestenbaum>Anyway, his previous attempt at conquering the world had not gone so well. He'd created these kind of cardboard models of famous landmarks that he tried to sell as kits.
<v David Kestenbaum>总之，他之前征服世界的尝试并不太顺利。他做了一些著名地标的纸板模型，试图作为套件出售。

00:10:49.100 --> 00:11:18.560
<v Peter Ocko>My mom and I and him-- I was an only child. And the three of us would go out to the garage with hairdryers and kind of shrink wrap these cardboard model kits and put them in UPS boxes. And that was our life for a few years. And then it just died. It just died. And so he was struggling because he saw himself as an idea guy. And so when he got the chance to go to Milton Bradley and be a game designer or a toy designer, I think he was super excited.
<v Peter Ocko>我和我妈还有他——我是独生子。我们三个会带上吹风机到车库，用热缩膜把这些纸板模型包起来，装进UPS盒子里。这就是我们几年的生活。然后生意就倒闭了。彻底倒闭了。所以他很挣扎，因为他一直把自己看作是“创意人才”。所以当他有机会去米尔顿·布拉德利担任游戏设计师或玩具设计师时，我觉得他超级兴奋。

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:27.950
<v David Kestenbaum>The one bad thing about the job was that Milton Bradley was 90 miles from where they lived in Massachusetts. So his dad would just work there the whole week, only come home on the weekends.
<v David Kestenbaum>这份工作唯一不好的一点是，米尔顿·布拉德利离他们在马萨诸塞州的家有90英里远。所以他父亲整个星期都待在那里工作，只有周末才回家。

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:47.270
<v Peter Ocko>I was in sixth grade. I hated it. I hated seeing him leave on a Sunday and then return the following week. And it was a long week, and I really missed him. So Fridays, when he pulled into the driveway, I was ecstatic. I just was so happy to see him. And he'd always come home with a box of stuff.
<v Peter Ocko>当时我在上六年级。我很讨厌这样。我讨厌看着他周日离开，然后下周才回来。那一周太漫长了，我真的很想他。所以每到周五，当他把车开进车道时，我都会欣喜若狂。我真的太高兴见到他了。而且他总是带一盒东西回家。

00:11:47.270 --> 00:12:10.970
<v David Kestenbaum>And the stuff in that box, it was kind of magical. This was 1977. And in that box were circuit boards and wires. Milton Bradley was making some of the first electronic games. Their big hit was Simon-- basically, an electronic version of Simon Says-- a kind of chunky Frisbee-shaped thing with four big buttons-- red, blue, green, yellow-- that would light up.
<v David Kestenbaum>盒子里装的东西，简直就像有魔力一样。那是1977年。盒子里装的是电路板和电线。米尔顿·布拉德利当时正在制作第一批电子游戏。他们的大热门是“西蒙”（Simon）——本质上是“西蒙说”的电子版——一种圆滚滚的飞盘状物体，上面有四个大按钮——红、蓝、绿、黄——会发光。

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:14.600
<v Peter Ocko>And it made these kind of square wave tones that were really distinctive.
<v Peter Ocko>它会发出那种独特的方波音调。

