00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:52.950
<v David Kestenbaum>OK. Even back then, it was a little boring. I'll be honest. I don't think there were any parents saying, like, I can't get my kid off the Simon. But it did feel like you were holding some object from the future, just to press the buttons and hear these kinds of sounds for the first time. Peter and his dad would sit around in this sort of attic room at this big table and just mess around with this stuff.
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧。即使在当时，这也有一点无聊。老实说。我不认为当时有家长会说，我没法让我孩子停下玩西蒙。但按下按钮并第一次听到这些声音，感觉确实像你拿着来自未来的物体。彼得和父亲会坐在阁楼房间里，围着大桌子摆弄这些东西。

00:12:52.950 --> 00:13:22.290
<v Peter Ocko>Opening the toys up for me and seeing the circuit board and understanding that this little circuit board was playing the game of Simon-- I would get in there with a screwdriver, pull it out, and then start playing around with it and changing the resistor here or there. You could make it speed up, and suddenly, it was generating electronic music at high speed and flashing lights. It just felt halfway between technology and magic, you know? And I think, for both of us, that was super exciting, you know.
<v Peter Ocko>对我来说，拆开玩具，看到电路板，明白这块小小的电路板在运行西蒙游戏——我会拿着螺丝起子钻进去，把它拆出来，然后开始玩它，修改这里的电阻或那里的零件。你可以让它加速，突然间，它就开始高速生成电子音乐并闪烁灯光。这感觉介于科技和魔法之间，你知道吗？我觉得，对我们俩来说，那都超级令人兴奋。

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:43.750
<v David Kestenbaum>They'd build little electronic kits together, which could get kind of tense. His dad liked to follow instructions-- Peter, not so much. They'd share little discoveries with each other, like when Peter realized you could take the spring from a ballpoint pen, put it across the terminals of a 9-volt battery and the electricity flowing through it would make it glow, like the filament in a light bulb.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们会一起组装小型电子套件，这有时会变得有点紧张。他父亲喜欢按说明书做——彼得可不太喜欢。他们会分享彼此的小发现，比如彼得意识到你可以取下圆珠笔里的弹簧，跨接到9伏电池的两端，流过的电流会让它发光，就像灯泡里的灯丝一样。

00:13:43.750 --> 00:14:06.850
<v David Kestenbaum>But more than anything, it was a way to spend time together. Week after week, when his dad would come home, this was their routine. They'd go up to the third floor and just be together, sitting side by side at this big drafting table. And then one day, as Peter thinks he remembers it, a kind of remarkable thing happened in that room, something that changed their lives. It was just Peter there that day in the workshop.
<v David Kestenbaum>但最重要的是，这是他们共度时光的一种方式。一周又一周，当他父亲回家时，这就是他们的日常。他们会去三楼，肩并肩坐在那张巨大的绘图桌前。然后有一天，据彼得记得，那个房间里发生了一件非同寻常的事，改变了他们一生的事。那天只有彼得在工作室里。

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:29.080
<v Peter Ocko>What I remember is he had gone back to work on a Monday morning and left behind a box of stuff. And in that box was a bunch of circuit boards for different things, including a Simon circuit board. And I remember pulling it out and connecting it up to some batteries and just tinkering around with it, playing around with it.
<v Peter Ocko>我记得的是，他周一早上回去工作了，留下了一盒东西。盒子里有一堆不同用途的电路板，包括一块西蒙电路板。我记得把它拿出来，连接上电池，然后就开始瞎摆弄，玩弄它。

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:53.740
<v Peter Ocko>And then I remember just looking at the lights and thinking, like, well, let me unsolder the lights and see if a motor will work there. Because we had these little hobby motors laying around. So I took off the four lights, and then I connected two motors to where two of the lights had been and tried it again. And the motor spun. When the lights would have lit up, the motor spun. And I was like, that's cool.
<v Peter Ocko>然后我记得看着那些灯光，心想，好吧，让我把灯拆下来，看看电机能不能在那个位置工作。因为我们手头有这些小爱好电机。所以我拆下了四个灯，然后在其中两个灯的位置接上了两个电机，又试了一次。电机转了。在灯原本应该亮起的地方，电机转了起来。我当时想，这太酷了。

00:14:53.740 --> 00:15:06.100
<v David Kestenbaum>In his memory, he then had another idea. I wonder if you could use those motors to make the wheels turn on a little car. Then the Simon board would be controlling a car. That would be neat.
<v David Kestenbaum>在他的记忆里，他随后又有了另一个想法。我在想能不能用这些电机让小车的轮子转动起来。然后西蒙电路板就能控制一辆车了。那一定会很棒。

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:16.330
<v Peter Ocko>There was a mode in the Simon game where you could tell it which lights you wanted. You could program the pattern yourself. So I was like, well, you could program left motor, right motor. And so it's like a programmable car.
<v Peter Ocko>西蒙游戏里有一个模式，你可以指定你想要的灯光顺序。你可以自己编程。所以我当时想，好吧，你可以编程“左电机，右电机”。所以它就像一辆可编程的小车。

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:25.900
<v David Kestenbaum>A programmable car. You could punch some buttons on it, tell it you wanted it to drive forward, turn right, drive some more, turn left. Then it would go and do it.
<v David Kestenbaum>一辆可编程的小车。你可以在上面按几个按钮，告诉它你想要它向前开，右转，再向前，左转。然后它就会真的去执行。

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:49.830
<v Peter Ocko>My dad came back on the following Friday, and I was excited to show him this little thing I'd built. And he'd took a look at it. It wasn't like the symphony started playing. He's like, that's really cool. That's a good idea. And I was like, cool. You think I have a good idea. That's awesome.
<v Peter Ocko>我父亲接下来的周五回来，我兴奋地给他看了我组装的这个小东西。他看了一眼。并不是像交响乐开始演奏那样。他只是说，这真的太酷了。这是个好主意。我当时想，酷。你认为我有好点子。那太棒了。

00:15:49.830 --> 00:16:15.600
<v David Kestenbaum>His dad brought the idea into work. And Milton Bradley started to develop it as an actual toy with Peter's dad leading the team. His dad would update him when he got home. Like, our little thing is still moving forward. And as it got further along, he'd bring home these kind of handmade prototypes of it. The toy that was taking form was sort of a tank, dump truck kind of thing. You could program it to carry something to a place and drop it off, all on its own.
<v David Kestenbaum>他父亲把这个点子带回了工作单位。米尔顿·布拉德利开始把它开发成真正的玩具，彼得的父亲负责团队。他父亲回家时会更新进度。比如，我们那个小东西还在继续推进。随着项目深入，他会带回家一些手工制作的原型机。正在形成的那个玩具大约是一辆坦克，又像自卸卡车。你可以给它编程，让它自主地把东西运到某个地方并卸下。

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:41.160
<v Peter Ocko>I remember one problem they had was they couldn't get the tank to go perfectly straight. I remember my dad having this eureka moment one day and being like, oh. And he put two magnets opposing each other on the end of the shaft that wasn't on the wheel so that the motors lined up with these magnets and the magnets grabbed each other without actually touching and kept the motors moving at exactly the same speed. It was brilliant.
<v Peter Ocko>我记得他们遇到的一个问题是，他们无法让坦克走得完全笔直。我记得有一天我父亲突然灵光一现，他说，噢。他在轴的末端放了两个相对的磁铁，这样电机就与这些磁铁对齐，磁铁在不接触的情况下相互吸引，让电机保持完全相同的速度转动。这太聪明了。

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:50.080
<v Peter Ocko>And he had those little victories. Imagine that over a nine-month period, where you just keep figuring it out, and then it works and then it's successful.
<v Peter Ocko>他有了那些小小的胜利时刻。想象一下在九个月的时间里，你不断地解决问题，然后它成功了，然后它变得大获成功。

