<v David Kestenbaum>Sales? Best estimate I was able to find-- $40 million.
<v David Kestenbaum>销量？我能找到的最佳估算——4000万美元。

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:40.590
<v David Kestenbaum>This project, it gave Peter's dad the thing he'd been wanting-- a real success. But people are complicated.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个项目给了彼得父亲他一直渴望的东西——真正的成功。但人是很复杂的。

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:53.300
<v Peter Ocko>He was on top of the world. And I know he felt good. He came home and felt good. But that started to sour as he started to realize how much money the company was going to make from this toy.
<v Peter Ocko>他当时处于世界之巅。我知道他感觉很好。他回家时感觉很好。但这开始变味了，当他意识到公司能从这个玩具里赚多少钱的时候。

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:56.420
<v David Kestenbaum>Because this was his ship that was supposed to come in.
<v David Kestenbaum>因为这就是他本以为会到来的那艘人生之船。

00:17:56.420 --> 00:18:25.730
<v Peter Ocko>I think he saw it-- at first, I think he was perfectly happy to be seen as the guy in charge of a project within a corporate framework that's doing well. And as the stakes got higher and the calls from his mom-- every Sunday, his mother would call. And she was never satisfied with where he was in life. I think she would have been really happy if he had been a doctor or a lawyer.
<v Peter Ocko>我觉得他看待这件事——起初，他对于被视为公司框架下项目负责人的感觉很满足。但随着赌注越来越高，来自他母亲的电话——他母亲每周日都会打电话。她对他生活的现状从来都不满意。我觉得如果他是个医生或律师，她会真的很高兴。

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:39.420
<v Peter Ocko>And I think for him, he started to see that he could prove himself with this project. And his self-worth, I think, became really tied up with the success of this project.
<v Peter Ocko>而对他来说，他开始意识到他可以通过这个项目证明自己。我想，他的自我价值感开始与这个项目的成功紧密相连。

00:18:39.420 --> 00:19:04.260
<v David Kestenbaum>Peter's dad was frustrated. He had brought this toy into existence. But in the end, it would be the company's success, not his. The toy was a big hit. But it wasn't going to make his dad rich. Peter would overhear his parents talking in the next room, his dad increasingly unhappy. And, realizing there wasn't going to be any glory for him, he quit the dream job.
<v David Kestenbaum>彼得的父亲很沮丧。他把这个玩具带到了世上。但最终，这是公司的成功，而不是他的。这个玩具大受欢迎。但它没能让他父亲发财。彼得会听到父母在隔壁房间谈话，他父亲越来越不高兴。意识到不会有属于自己的荣誉，他辞去了那份梦想的工作。

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:29.730
<v David Kestenbaum>He thought about legal action. But, of course, he couldn't sue Milton Bradley for taking his idea. He was an employee, so the company owned everything he created, which is pretty much how it works at any company. But at some point, Peter's dad talks to a lawyer. And one of the things he says to the lawyer is, hey, the whole idea for Big Trak, I didn't come up with that. It actually came from my son, from Peter, who was not a Milton Bradley employee.
<v David Kestenbaum>他考虑过诉诸法律。但当然，他不能因为米尔顿·布拉德利采用了他的想法而起诉他们。他是员工，所以公司拥有他创造的一切，这在任何公司都是如此。但在某个时候，彼得的父亲咨询了一位律师。他跟律师说的一件事是：嘿，关于Big Trak的整个点子，那不是我想出来的。其实是我儿子彼得想出来的，他不是米尔顿·布拉德利的员工。

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:35.090
<v Peter Ocko>Then the lawyer says to him, well, a kid can sue a corporation.
<v Peter Ocko>然后律师告诉他，嗯，孩子是可以起诉公司的。

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.220
<v David Kestenbaum>A kid can sue a corporation.
<v David Kestenbaum>孩子可以起诉公司。

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:56.690
<v Peter Ocko>A kid can sue a corporation. And I know this because my mom had kept diaries her whole life. And I found the diary from the time this was all happening. And she had just noted in there, Steve says, lawyer said, a kid can sue a corporation.
<v Peter Ocko>孩子可以起诉公司。我知道这一点是因为我妈妈一直有写日记的习惯。我找到了事情发生那段时间的日记。她随手记录道：“史蒂夫说，律师说，孩子可以起诉公司。”

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:20.640
<v David Kestenbaum>And that is how the lawsuit Peter Ocko, 13-year-old boy, versus the Milton Bradley company came to be. The basis for the lawsuit was that if Peter, the kid, had come up with the initial idea for this multi-million-dollar toy, then he was owed royalties. It could be a lot of money.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是后来“13岁男孩彼得·奥克起诉米尔顿·布拉德利公司”一案的由来。诉讼的基础是，如果彼得这个孩子想出了这个数百万美元玩具的原始创意，那么他就应得版税。那可能是一大笔钱。

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:33.960
<v David Kestenbaum>I asked Peter if the prospect of being a kid suing a giant corporation felt scary. He said not really, though maybe it should have. He said it felt thrilling, kind of awesome. A big, fancy Boston law firm took their case.
<v David Kestenbaum>我问彼得，作为孩子起诉巨型公司是否感到害怕。他说并不觉得，虽然也许本该如此。他说那感觉很刺激，甚至很酷。一家大型的高端波士顿律师事务所接手了他们的案子。

00:20:33.960 --> 00:21:00.390
<v Peter Ocko>And that firm decides, we're going to take this case on contingency. We actually think this has a really good chance. And you guys won't have to pay anything but the court costs or the Xerox costs. So at this point, my dad is ecstatic. And we're both ecstatic. I'm now playing a really important role in the both of us getting what's due for this idea.
<v Peter Ocko>那家公司决定，我们要风险代理这个案子。我们确实认为胜算很大。你们不需要支付任何费用，只需承担诉讼成本或复印费。所以这时，我父亲欣喜若狂。我们也都很兴奋。我现在在这一过程中扮演了非常重要的角色，为了我们俩争取这个创意应得的东西。

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:09.070
<v David Kestenbaum>Peter says, looking back, he was just coming into that age where you're really craving some kind of identity. And now he had one, teenage whiz kid.
<v David Kestenbaum>彼得说，回头看，他当时正处于渴望某种身份认同的年龄。而现在他拥有了一个：少年天才。

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:27.970
<v Peter Ocko>I'm just about to go into high school. And, suddenly, I have to take time off from school to go meet with lawyers and give a deposition. And now everybody at my school knows what's going on. And it's really exciting. It's like, I'm at the center of it.
<v Peter Ocko>我当时刚要上高中。突然间，我得请假去见律师，接受取证。现在我学校里的每个人都知道发生了什么。这太令人兴奋了。就像我处于事件的中心。

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:48.430
<v David Kestenbaum>Two years went by of preparation, legal back and forth. And, finally, the court date approached. Peter and his dad would take long walks around the block with the dog, both kind of excited by it all, going over the story he would tell in court. And then there was one night, the night Peter's thought about and puzzled over for years.
<v David Kestenbaum>两年的筹备、法律交锋过去了。最后，开庭日期临近了。彼得和父亲会带着狗在街区散步，两人都对此感到兴奋，温习着他在法庭上要讲的故事。然后有一个晚上，彼得多年来一直反复回想和困惑的那个晚上。

00:21:48.430 --> 00:22:05.470
<v Peter Ocko>We had a dinner conversation that changed my life. I'm not trying to be dramatic. I just-- it's like, I look back at the little pieces that had to move into place for this all to go south. And they're so small.
<v Peter Ocko>我们发生了一次改变我人生的晚餐谈话。我不是想装得戏剧化。我只是——回头看，那些必须归位的小碎片最终让这一切走向了溃败。它们太微小了。

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:34.330
<v David Kestenbaum>To understand what happened, you need to know two things. First was that before bringing the lawsuit, Peter's dad had signed a document making it so that, legally, he no longer had financial responsibility for Peter. This was at the recommendation of the lawyers and made it so that, as far as the jury would be concerned, this would be about Peter, not his dad trying to get money through him-- even though it kind of was. It would also help protect any money they won. That's the first thing you need to know. Here's the other thing.
<v David Kestenbaum>要理解发生了什么，你需要知道两件事。第一是，在提起诉讼前，彼得的父亲签署了一份文件，在法律上让他不再承担对彼得的经济责任。这是律师的建议，这样在陪审团看来，这就是关于彼得个人的案子，而不是他父亲想通过他赚钱——尽管本质上确实是这样。这也将有助于保护他们赢来的任何钱。这是你需要知道的第一件事。还有一件事。

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:53.760
<v Peter Ocko>My mother and father didn't have a smooth marriage, and that's an understatement. They fought a lot. By the time I was born-- and, certainly, by the time I was a teenager-- they spent a lot of time and energy trying to upset each other.
<v Peter Ocko>我父母的婚姻并不顺遂，这还是保守的说法。他们经常吵架。到我出生时——当然到我十几岁时——他们花了很多时间和精力试图让对方难受。

00:22:53.760 --> 00:23:18.000
<v Peter Ocko>And if you put that into the soup, along with the fact that my mother now has taken financial control of me and I am the plaintiff in this lawsuit-- we all sit down to dinner soon after that agreement my mom signed. And we're sitting around the table. It's the three of us-- it's a tiny family. We're all sitting there, staring at each other.
<v Peter Ocko>如果你把这些放进汤里，再加上我母亲现在已经接管了我的经济控制权，而我是这起诉讼的原告——我们在那份协议签署后坐下来吃饭。我们坐在桌边。只有我们三个——一个微小的家庭。我们坐在那儿，面面相觑。

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:35.760
<v Peter Ocko>And my mom says something to the effect of, well, if you win now, Peter, this money will be yours to control. I'm just your guardian, but it's not the family's. It's really yours. And I think she knew exactly what she was saying. She was absolutely using it to get under my dad's skin.
<v Peter Ocko>我妈妈说了一句大意是：好吧，如果你现在赢了，彼得，这些钱将由你控制。我只是你的监护人，但这不是家庭的钱。这真的是你的。我想她很清楚自己在说什么。她绝对是用这句话来激怒我父亲。

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:57.430
<v Peter Ocko>And my dad was not a drinker. But if he had a beer or two, he would get very grumpy. And he'd had a couple of beers. And he looks at her. And then I-- sort of missing what's going on-- pick it up. And I'm like, yeah, Dad, maybe I'll share the money with you if I win. But it really is-- I am the plaintiff.
<v Peter Ocko>而且我父亲不是个爱喝酒的人。但如果他喝了一两杯啤酒，他就会变得非常暴躁。他刚好喝了几杯。他看着她。然后我——有点没明白发生了什么——接过了话茬。我说，是啊，爸爸，如果我赢了，我也许会跟你分钱。但确实是——我才是原告。

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:21.320
<v Peter Ocko>And I don't know. I guess if you could see a fuse blow in my father's head, you would have seen it. Because he kind of took a breath. He said, you know, the real truth, Peter, is that I invented Big Trak, not you. I just told you you did it so that we could bring this lawsuit.
<v Peter Ocko>我不知道。我想如果你能看到我父亲脑子里那根保险丝烧断的样子，你肯定能看到。因为他深吸了一口气。他说，你知道吗，彼得，真相其实是我发明了Big Trak，不是你。我只是告诉你那是你做的，这样我们才能提起这场诉讼。

00:24:21.320 --> 00:25:02.300
<v Peter Ocko>And I just remember the bottom falling out. And I don't know. At that moment, to be-- I think I was 14-- and have my dad look at me and say that, it was amazing how easy it was for me to question everything and just feel like everything that had led up to that moment was a bunch of BS. There was something about him staring at me and saying that that I just doubted everything.
<v Peter Ocko>我只记得那一刻感觉天塌了。我不知道。在那一刻——我想我当时14岁——看着我父亲跟我说那种话，能让我那么轻易地质疑一切，感觉之前导致那一刻的一切都是一堆胡扯。他盯着我说的那些话，让我对所有事情都产生了怀疑。

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:21.010
<v David Kestenbaum>Like that moment he remembered, hooking the Simon board up to the motors, when exactly did that happen? Was it really before his dad had started on the project at work? Or was he doing it after his dad began working on Big Trak? Like, maybe what he remembered was him trying to copy the thing his dad was doing.
<v David Kestenbaum>比如他记得的那一刻，把西蒙电路板连到电机上，那到底是什么时候发生的？是真的在他父亲开始工作项目之前吗？还是在他父亲开始研究Big Trak之后？也许他记得的是他试图模仿父亲正在做的事情。

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:33.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Also, at this point, this was a family story they had told many times. Maybe it had grown in the telling, and the telling had created its own version in his head. He had all these doubts.
<v David Kestenbaum>而且到这个地步，这是一个他们讲过很多次的家庭故事。也许在讲述中它不断生长，而讲述本身在他脑海里创造了它自己的版本。他心中充满了怀疑。

00:25:33.400 --> 00:26:04.120
<v Peter Ocko>I'm not a whiz kid. I've been used. I don't know. It's really hard for me to talk about. I guess what I felt at the moment was outrage because I believed that I had done it-- but also, at the same exact moment, tremendous doubt. I remember going later to my mom that night and being like, what's going on? What do you remember?
<v Peter Ocko>我不是什么天才少年。我被利用了。我不知道。这真的很难谈论。我想我那一刻感觉到的是愤怒，因为我相信那是我做的——但同时，也感到了巨大的怀疑。我记得当晚后来去问我妈，到底怎么回事？你记得什么？

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:21.260
<v Peter Ocko>And the thing that drove me crazy was, rather than instantly say, like, oh, that's a terrible thing your dad did. Like, he's just in a bad place-- or whatever I would have wished her to say-- instead, she got super vague. And she's like, I don't know what happened.
<v Peter Ocko>让我抓狂的是，她没有立刻说：噢，你爸做的事太糟糕了。就像，他只是陷入了困境——或者任何我希望她说的话——相反，她变得非常含糊。她说，我不知道发生了什么。

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:30.920
<v David Kestenbaum>The court date got closer and closer. His dad had said this confusing and upsetting thing. Peter didn't know what to make of it. And he was all alone with that.
<v David Kestenbaum>开庭日期越来越近了。他父亲说了那番令人困惑和不安的话。彼得不知道该怎么办。而且他独自承受这一切。

00:26:30.920 --> 00:27:04.610
<v Peter Ocko>The hardest part about it was there was no third-party grown-up to weigh in and help me. I couldn't tell the lawyer, hey, listen, my dad said this. What do you think? I had to keep that absolutely to myself and move forward. And my dad did the same thing and my mom did the same thing. And we kind of moved on from that dinner conversation as if nothing had happened.
<v Peter Ocko>最困难的部分是没有任何第三方大人来介入并帮我。我不能去跟律师说，嘿，听着，我爸说了这个。你觉得呢？我必须把这件事严严实实藏在心里继续前行。我父亲也一样，我母亲也一样。我们像是没发生过什么一样从那次晚餐谈话中走了出来。

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:39.540
<v Peter Ocko>I would be in public with my dad. And we would be telling the story of the trial and talking about how excited we were. And he would be smiling with genuine pride at the fact that his kid was the reason he was able to sue the company and we were going to get justice. Like, he was genuinely proud of me in those moments. And then we'd be alone together, and I'd bring up the conversation. He just couldn't talk about it.
<v Peter Ocko>我会和父亲出现在公众场合。我们会讲述审判的故事，谈论我们有多兴奋。而他会带着真实的自豪微笑，因为他的孩子是他能够起诉公司的原因，我们将要获得公正。在那些时刻，他确实为我感到骄傲。然后我们单独待在一起，我提起那次对话。他就是没法谈论。

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:55.150
<v David Kestenbaum>The trial finally happened. His dad, very invested in it, hated the opposing lawyer, saw him as truly evil. Peter says the whole trial, he wasn't really wondering if they would win. He was looking for clues to what his dad had said and what the truth was.
<v David Kestenbaum>审判终于开始了。他父亲非常投入，讨厌对方律师，认为他是真正的恶魔。彼得说在整个审判过程中，他并没有真的在想会不会赢。他在寻找线索，想知道他父亲说的是什么，真相到底是什么。

00:27:55.150 --> 00:28:15.390
<v David Kestenbaum>Eventually, Peter took the stand and swore on the Bible. He says, at that moment, it was the part of him that believed it swearing the oath. He told the story he remembered at that moment in their little workroom at home. His lawyer had him take apart a Simon with a screwdriver on the stand, pull out the circuit board, which seemed to impress the jury.
<v David Kestenbaum>最终，彼得走上证人席并在圣经上宣誓。他说，那一刻，是他心中相信真相的那部分在宣誓。他讲述了他在家里那间小屋子里记得的那一刻。律师让他当庭用螺丝起子拆开一个西蒙，取出电路板，这似乎让陪审团印象深刻。

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:36.060
<v David Kestenbaum>It's worth noting that the trial was around the time the movie WarGames came out. That's the one where a kid saves the world from nuclear catastrophe because he understands the computers controlling the nuclear weapons better than the adults do. Not surprisingly, the press showed up to the trial. The headline writes itself. "Teenager Sues Milton Bradley."
<v David Kestenbaum>值得一提的是，审判期间刚好是电影《战争游戏》（WarGames）上映的时候。那部电影讲的是一个孩子拯救世界免于核灾难，因为他比成年人更了解控制核武器的计算机。不出所料，媒体出现在审判现场。标题自己就写好了。“少年起诉米尔顿·布拉德利”。

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:48.110
<v Peter Ocko>Let's see. "Dedham, Mass-- a 16-year-old high school boy from Brookline claims that the toymaker Milton Bradley company owes him royalties for a robot tank he claims he invented when he was 12."
<v Peter Ocko>来看看。“马萨诸塞州戴德姆——一名来自布鲁克林的16岁高中生称，玩具制造商米尔顿·布拉德利公司欠他版税，因为他声称在12岁时发明了一辆机器人坦克。”

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:49.520
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh my god, that's you.
<v David Kestenbaum>天哪，那是你。

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:51.690
<v Peter Ocko>That's me.
<v Peter Ocko>是我。

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:54.950
<v David Kestenbaum>OK, now read the other headline.
<v David Kestenbaum>OK，现在看另一个标题。

00:28:54.950 --> 00:29:21.980
<v Peter Ocko>"Royalties denied to boy who claims toy his idea. A 16-year-old high school student who claimed he invented a toy robot tank at the age of 12 has lost his bid for $2 million in royalties from Milton Bradley company. A Norfolk Superior Court jury decided there was no valid written contract between the toymaker and Peter Ocko, son of Stephen Ocko, who worked until 1978 as a senior game designer for Milton Bradley."
<v Peter Ocko>“声称玩具是自己创意的男孩被拒绝版税。一名16岁的高中生声称在12岁时发明了一辆机器人坦克，却在向米尔顿·布拉德利公司索取200万美元版税的诉讼中败诉。诺福克高等法院陪审团裁定，玩具制造商与彼得·奥克（Stephen Ocko之子，1978年前任米尔顿·布拉德利高级游戏设计师）之间不存在有效的书面合同。”

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:24.050
<v David Kestenbaum>So you guys lost.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以你们输了。

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:25.610
<v Peter Ocko>We did.
<v Peter Ocko>我们输了。

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:28.770
<v David Kestenbaum>What do you remember about that day?
<v David Kestenbaum>你还记得那天吗？

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:31.680
<v Peter Ocko>Ugh, dark day, literally. It was raining.
<v Peter Ocko>呃，那是个黑暗的日子，字面意义上的。下着雨。

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:41.100
<v David Kestenbaum>After the verdict came down, they went out the back of the courthouse to avoid the press, the three of them-- mom, dad, and him. There was a phone booth on the way out.
<v David Kestenbaum>判决下来后，他们从法院后门走出去躲避记者，他们三个——妈妈、爸爸和我。路上有个电话亭。

00:29:41.100 --> 00:30:08.420
<v Peter Ocko>And my dad-- because he couldn't wait-- stepped into one and called his mom. And then he came out, and we all just got in the car. We drove home. And we pulled into the driveway. And we got out of the car. And my dad just kind of walked to the porch. And he sat down on the steps. And he just burst into tears. And I'd never really seen him cry before.
<v Peter Ocko>我爸爸——因为他迫不及待了——走进电话亭给他妈打了电话。然后他出来，我们都上了车。我们开车回家。进了车道。我们从车上下来。我爸爸只是走到门廊。他坐在台阶上。然后他突然泪流满面。我之前从未真正见过他哭。

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:10.540
<v David Kestenbaum>Wow.
<v David Kestenbaum>哇。

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:38.180
<v Peter Ocko>And it was hard. But I do remember feeling a weight lifted-- not just that the trial was over and we didn't have to worry about that, but that all the stuff that had swirled around the truth and the real truth and my dad and me-- that we could just move on.
<v Peter Ocko>那是很难过的。但我确实记得感到一种重担卸下了——不仅是审判结束了，我们不用担心那个了，而且是所有围绕真相、真正真相、我和我爸之间的问题——我们可以继续前行。

00:30:38.180 --> 00:31:02.540
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm pretty sure this isn't the goal of the US legal system. But losing this case, it made their lives better. Peter says losing saved them from being the worst versions of themselves, freed them from something bad. And, like three programmable cars, everyone was on a new trajectory. His dad turned left. His mom went right. They got divorced. Peter finished high school and moved away to college.
<v David Kestenbaum>我很确定这不是美国法律体系的目标。但输掉这场官司确实让他们的生活变好了。彼得说，输了使他们避免成为最糟糕的自己，从某种坏事中解脱了出来。就像三辆可编程的小车，每个人都踏上了新的轨道。他父亲向左转。他母亲向右走。他们离婚了。彼得高中毕业，去了大学。

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:34.250
<v David Kestenbaum>His dad began a whole new life after that where he seemed much more at peace. He remarried, got a degree in education, worked with kids and LEGOs, inventing stuff. But that moment, it really broke something. Peter says there was always a hint of competitiveness between him and his dad. It would come up when they talked about where he might go to college. Was his school better than his dad's? Or when he was little when they'd roughhouse-- they both kind of wanted to win. There was a tinge of aggression to it, he says.
<v David Kestenbaum>在那之后，他父亲开启了全新的生活，看起来内心平静多了。他再婚了，获得了教育学位，开始和孩子们一起玩乐高，发明东西。但那一刻，确实弄坏了一些东西。彼得说他和父亲之间总有一丝竞争感。当谈到他可能去哪所大学时，这种感觉就会出现。他的学校比他父亲的好吗？或者当他小的时候，他们打闹时——他们俩其实都想赢。他说这其中带有一种攻击性的色彩。

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:49.880
<v David Kestenbaum>And that night at dinner, it was like it flipped some switch between them. Those moments they used to have hanging around in the workroom, happily tinkering together, those were gone. And what was left was a feeling that they were not exactly on the same team anymore.
<v David Kestenbaum>而那晚的晚餐就像在他们之间拨动了一个开关。那些他们过去在工作室里一起快乐地摆弄东西的时光消失了。留下的是一种感觉：他们再也不是同一战线的人了。

00:31:49.880 --> 00:32:20.510
<v Peter Ocko>It was this kind of lingering sense that we had been thrust into something that made us adversaries and competitors, and we never came out of it. And I came to hate that. I hated that when I would achieve something in my life, like get a new job or whatever, I would hesitate telling my dad because it almost seemed to wound him.
<v Peter Ocko>这是一种萦绕不散的感觉，我们被推入了一件让我们变成对手和竞争者的事情中，而我们再也没能从中走出来。我讨厌那种感觉。我讨厌当我生活中取得成就，比如找了份新工作什么的，我会犹豫要不要告诉爸爸，因为这似乎会伤害到他。

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:37.610
<v Peter Ocko>What we had been through I think had changed both of us and kind of broke something that we never got back. We never got back an empathy.
<v Peter Ocko>我想我们经历过的一切改变了我们俩，弄坏了一些我们再也找不回来的东西。我们再也没找回那种同理心。

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:54.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Peter is pretty sure he knows what the truth is about who came up with the idea for Big Trak. Maybe it was clear at the time. But it was hard to see as a kid-- hard to feel it with confidence, anyway. The truth-- the only thing that really makes sense is-- that Peter had come up with the idea for the toy.
<v David Kestenbaum>彼得非常确定他知道Big Trak的创意到底归谁。也许当时很清楚。但作为一个孩子很难看清——至少很难自信地感受到。真相——唯一真正说得通的是——彼得确实想出了那个玩具的创意。

00:32:54.980 --> 00:33:20.880
<v David Kestenbaum>In the trial, some of his dad's coworkers testified that he had come into work and said as much. And it was a thing that, as a family, they had talked about before the lawsuit, that this little idea Peter had was being turned into an actual toy and how cool that was. But that night at dinner, it was a hard fact for his father to have thrown in his face. And so he said what he said. His dad's way of moving past it was just to keep moving.
<v David Kestenbaum>在审判中，他父亲的一些同事出庭作证说，他确实去单位说过这些。而且这是他们作为家庭在诉讼前就谈论过的事，彼得这个小小的创意正在变成一个真正的玩具，那太酷了。但那天晚上晚餐时，这对父亲来说是一个难以面对的残酷事实。所以他说了那番话。他父亲跨过这件事的方式就是不断前行。

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:27.390
<v David Kestenbaum>Years past. Peter had kids. His dad would come and visit-- really enjoyed being a grandfather.
<v David Kestenbaum>岁月流逝。彼得有了孩子。他父亲会来看望——真的非常享受当爷爷。

00:33:27.390 --> 00:34:08.180
<v Peter Ocko>And I remember thinking, like, this is the dad I remember before that trial. Before all of that started, I remember a kind, sharing, open, curious guy. And it was nice to kind of revisit that. But I would occasionally say to him, do you ever want to talk about this? Because I think about it a lot. And the few times I would bring it up, he would just block. He would just say, I don't. I can't. There's nothing to talk about.
<v Peter Ocko>我记得我在想，这就是我审判前记得的那个爸爸。在这一切开始前，我记得一个善良、乐于分享、开明、好奇的人。能重温那种感觉真好。但我偶尔会问他：你还想谈谈那件事吗？因为我经常想起它。那几次我提起时，他就会阻断。他只会说：我不谈。我做不到。没什么好谈的。

00:34:08.180 --> 00:34:15.090
<v David Kestenbaum>Why do you think he wouldn't just tell you which was the truth?
<v David Kestenbaum>你为什么觉得他不告诉你哪个才是真相？

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:35.760
<v Peter Ocko>I don't know. Part of me feels that he did something that he was ashamed of and just couldn't talk about that piece of it.
<v Peter Ocko>我不知道。我心里觉得他做了一件让他感到羞愧的事，只是无法谈论那部分。

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:43.920
<v David Kestenbaum>The lawsuit did come up one last time, in a very strange way, almost like the universe was trying to put the pieces back together.
<v David Kestenbaum>那场诉讼最后还出现过一次，以一种非常奇怪的方式，就像宇宙试图把碎片重新拼凑起来。

00:34:43.920 --> 00:35:06.840
<v Peter Ocko>After my dad got cancer, he came out a few times to visit. And one of those times, we had a great visit. And I took him to LAX. And it was back when you could go to the gate with people. And I went to the gate with him. And we were sitting at the gate. And I hear this voice across the room. And it's the attorney from Milton Bradley.
<v Peter Ocko>我爸爸得癌症后，他来看过我几次。其中一次，我们相处得非常愉快。我带他去洛杉矶国际机场（LAX）。那时候你还可以带人去登机口。我和他一起去了登机口。我们坐在登机口。我听到屋子对面有一个声音。是米尔顿·布拉德利的那位律师。

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:07.500
<v David Kestenbaum>No way.
<v David Kestenbaum>不会吧。

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:35.450
<v Peter Ocko>And he's standing there with a younger woman-- I'm assuming his daughter. And my dad just tenses up because he sees it, and I see it. And I don't know why. But I just stood up, and I walked over to the guy. And I shake his hand, and I introduce myself. And he's like, oh, Peter, how are you? And I say, oh, I'm fine. My dad's here. And he's like, oh, wonderful.
<v Peter Ocko>他正站在那儿，身边还有一个年轻女性——我猜是他女儿。我爸爸看到他立刻变得紧张起来，我也看到了。我不知道为什么。但我只是站起身，走过去找那个人。我跟他握了手，做了自我介绍。他说，噢，彼得，你好吗？我说，我很好。我爸在那儿。他说了句，噢，太棒了。

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:56.090
<v Peter Ocko>And he walks with me over to my father. And my father at this point is kind of a deer in the headlights. And I helped my dad stand up. And they shake hands. And I mentioned, I live in Los Angeles. And he says, yeah, my daughter's out here now. And he goes, oh, I always knew you'd be a success, Peter. That's wonderful. And my dad said, yep, I'm really proud of him.
<v Peter Ocko>他和我一起走向我父亲。我父亲那一刻像只受惊的鹿一样。我帮我爸站起来。他们握了手。我提到我就住在洛杉矶。他说，对，我女儿现在也在这儿。然后他说道，噢，彼得，我一直都知道你会成功的。那太棒了。我爸说，没错，我真的很为他骄傲。

00:35:56.090 --> 00:36:20.840
<v Peter Ocko>And then we just sort of said goodbye. And we sat down. And the attorney got called to first class and went on board. And then we sat there and didn't really say much. And then my dad got called on to coach, and off he went. And that was one of the last times I saw him.
<v Peter Ocko>然后我们只是道了别。我们坐了下来。那位律师被叫去头等舱登机了。我们坐在那儿，没怎么多说话。然后我爸被叫去登经济舱，他离开了。那是我最后几次见到他中的一次。

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:33.590
<v David Kestenbaum>The earliest known version of the Humpty Dumpty rhyme, it's not the same as the one you know. It may have been a riddle about an egg, or it may have been a poem about a person. It went like this.
<v David Kestenbaum>已知的最早版本的矮胖子童谣，和你所知的不太一样。它可能是一个关于蛋的谜语，也可能是一首关于人的诗。它是这样写的：

00:36:33.590 --> 00:37:00.210
<v David Kestenbaum>"Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall. Humpty Dumpty had a great fall. Four-score men and four-score more could not make Humpty Dumpty where he was before--" which I read differently from the one we know. You can put a broken thing back together. It's just going to be different.
<v David Kestenbaum>“矮胖子坐在墙头上。矮胖子重重地摔了一跤。八十个大汉加八十个，也无法让矮胖子恢复原样——”这和我们所知的版本读起来很不一样。你可以把破碎的东西重新拼在一起。它只是会变得不一样。

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:20.580
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is our senior editor. Coming up, a man-- not an egg-- falls off a very high wall. And he does not look around for either king's horses or king's men. Imagine that. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们的资深编辑。接下来，一个男人——不是一个蛋——从一堵非常高的墙上摔了下来。他没有指望国王的马或国王的仆人。想象一下。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:29.610
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "All the King's Horses"-- about things broken so badly, they cannot be put back together. We've arrived at act two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目《国王所有的马》——关于那些破碎得太严重、无法恢复原样的东西。我们到达了节目的第二幕。

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:48.630
<v Ira Glass>Act two, The 95. This next story is about somebody examining something very broken, something documented in a list assembled by the Committee to Protect Journalists, of journalists who've been killed in the Israel-Hamas war. It's the largest number of journalists who've died in any conflict since they started counting.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“95”。下一个故事是关于某人审视破碎的东西，记录在保护记者委员会（Committee to Protect Journalists）的一份名单中，记录的是在以色列-哈马斯战争中遇难的记者。这是他们开始统计以来，在任何冲突中死亡记者人数最多的一次。

00:37:48.630 --> 00:38:08.010
<v Ira Glass>The list gives each person's name, where they worked, circumstances of their death-- sometimes a few more details, but often, that's all. Reporter Dana Ballout has been reading about the people on the list, watching their work, talking to their colleagues, trying to piece together who they were and what was lost when they died. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>名单列出了每个人的名字、工作单位、死亡经过——有时多几条细节，但通常也就这样。记者达娜·巴鲁特（Dana Ballout）一直在阅读名单上的人，观看他们的作品，采访他们的同事，试图拼凑出他们是谁，以及他们死时失去了什么。她在那儿。

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:22.550
<v Dana Ballout>I first started noticing the journalists dying on Instagram. I'm a journalist. I'm Arab. And I've reported on war. A big part of my community is other Arab journalists who do the same thing. And when someone dies, news travels fast.
<v Dana Ballout>我最初是在Instagram上注意到记者死亡的消息。我是记者。我是阿拉伯人。我报道过战争。我的社区很大一部分是做同样事情的其他阿拉伯记者。当有人去世，消息传播得很快。

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:42.890
<v Dana Ballout>Recently, I pulled up the list that the Committee to Protect Journalists has been keeping and looked at it for the first time. There are 95 journalists and media workers on it as of today. Almost everyone on it is Palestinian. Scrolling through, I started to get angry. These were the people carrying the burden of documenting this whole war.
<v Dana Ballout>最近，我调出了保护记者委员会一直在保存的名单，第一次看了看。截至今天，名单上有95名记者和媒体工作者。上面几乎每个人都是巴勒斯坦人。翻阅着，我开始愤怒。这些人承担着记录整场战争的重担。

00:38:42.890 --> 00:39:01.610
<v Dana Ballout>Israel is not allowing foreign journalists into Gaza, except on rare occasions with military escorts. These people's names are being buried in a giant list that keeps growing. What I want to do is lift some of them off the list for a moment and give you a glimpse of who they were and the work they made.
<v Dana Ballout>以色列不允许外国记者进入加沙，除了偶尔有军事护送的特殊情况。这些人的名字正被埋没在一个不断增长的巨大名单里。我想做的是把他们中的一些人从名单上提出来，让你一瞥他们是谁，以及他们创造的作品。

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:15.170
<v Dana Ballout>I'll start with Sari Mansour. Sari was the director of Al Quds News Network. And he posted a 22-second video on November 18 that was a report from the war, but it also gave me a picture into his marriage.
<v Dana Ballout>先从萨里·曼苏尔（Sari Mansour）开始。萨里是圣城新闻网（Al Quds News Network）的负责人。他在11月18日发了一段22秒的视频，那是一篇关于战争的报道，但也让我看到了他的婚姻生活。

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:21.110
<v Sari Mansour>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sari Mansour>[阿拉伯语演讲]

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:41.140
<v Dana Ballout>Sari's wearing his press vest and looks exhausted. He's explaining that cell service and the internet keep getting cut off and it's often impossible to text or call anyone, including his wife. So they've resorted to using handwritten letters to communicate while he's out reporting, sending them back and forth with neighbors or colleagues.
<v Dana Ballout>萨里穿着他的媒体马甲，看起来筋疲力尽。他解释说手机服务和互联网一直中断，通常不可能发短信或打电话给任何人，包括他的妻子。所以他们不得不诉诸于手写信件来交流，他在外面报道时，通过邻居或同事把信件传回去。

00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:47.250
<v Sari Mansour>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sari Mansour>[阿拉伯语演讲]

00:39:47.250 --> 00:40:03.960
<v Dana Ballout>He ends the video with a picture of one of these letters from his wife. In it, she writes, "Me and the kids stayed up waiting for you until the morning, and you didn't come home. We were really sad. I kept telling the kids, look, he's coming. But you didn't show up. May God forgive you.
<v Dana Ballout>他以妻子写的一封信的照片结束了视频。信中她写道：“我和孩子们一直等着你等到早上，你没回家。我们真的很难过。我一直在告诉孩子们，看，他要回来了。但你没出现。愿上帝宽恕你。

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:26.470
<v Dana Ballout>Come home tomorrow and eat with us. Do you want me to make you kebab or maybe kabseh? Bring your friends with you. It's OK. And give Aziz the battery to charge. What do you think about me sending you handwritten letters with messenger pigeons from now on? Ha, ha, ha. I'm just kidding. I want to curse at you, but we're living in a war. Too bad. OK, I love you. Bye."
<v Dana Ballout>明天回来和我们一起吃饭吧。你想让我给你做烤肉串还是卡布萨？带上你的朋友吧。没关系。把电池给阿齐兹（Aziz）充电。你觉得我以后用信鸽给你寄手写信怎么样？哈哈，开玩笑的。我想骂你，但我们正生活在战争中。太遗憾了。好吧，爱你。再见。”

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:44.200
<v Dana Ballout>A few hours after he shared that letter, Sari and his coworker, Hassouneh Salim, were at Sari's home when they were killed by an Israeli airstrike that hit his house. His wife and kids, who weren't there, survived.
<v Dana Ballout>在他分享那封信几个小时后，萨里和他的同事哈苏内·萨利姆（Hassouneh Salim）在萨里的家中被以色列的空袭炸死。他的妻子和孩子当时不在那里，幸免于难。

00:40:44.200 --> 00:41:04.520
<v Dana Ballout>Gaza's tiny, and the journalist community is really close. Reading the list, you can see all the connections between people, like with Ibrahim Lafi. Ibrahim was a photo journalist, one of the first journalists to die. He was killed while reporting on October 7. He was just 21, still new to journalism.
<v Dana Ballout>加沙很小，记者圈子真的很亲密。读着名单，你可以看到人与人之间的所有联系，比如易卜拉欣·拉菲（Ibrahim Lafi）。易卜拉欣是一名摄影记者，最早死亡的记者之一。他在10月7日报道时遇难。他才21岁，刚入行。

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:21.010
<v Dana Ballout>On his Instagram, you can see that in his posts just a few years ago, he was still practicing his photography, taking pictures of coffee cups and flowers. Then he started doing beautiful portraits and action shots. You can really feel him starting to become a journalist.
<v Dana Ballout>在他的Instagram上，你可以看到他在几年前的帖子还在练习摄影，拍咖啡杯和花。然后他开始拍美丽的肖像和动作镜头。你真的能感觉到他开始成为一名记者。

00:41:21.010 --> 00:41:42.020
<v Dana Ballout>Clicking around on Instagram, I found a tribute post about Ibrahim from his coworker, Roshdi Serraj. In this photo, Ibrahim's staring intently at the back of a camera, his face lit up by the light from the viewfinder. He looks so young. The caption reads, "My assistant is gone. Ibrahim is gone."
<v Dana Ballout>点击Instagram，我发现了他的同事罗什迪·塞拉杰（Roshdi Serraj）发布的关于易卜拉欣的纪念贴。在这张照片里，易卜拉欣正凝视着相机的背面，他的脸被取景器透出的光照亮。他看起来太年轻了。配文写着：“我的助手走了。易卜拉欣走了。”

00:41:42.020 --> 00:42:09.330
<v Dana Ballout>Roshdi himself was a beloved journalist and filmmaker. And I know that because he's also on the list. He was killed just two weeks after Ibrahim. I read the tribute post to him too. I saw this over and over again, journalists posting tributes, who were then killed themselves soon after. And a tribute goes up for them. And then the pattern continues.
<v Dana Ballout>罗什迪本人也是一位受人爱戴的记者和电影制作人。我知道这一点是因为他也在这份名单上。他在易卜拉欣去世后两周被杀。我也读了他的纪念贴。我一次又一次地看到这种情况，记者发布悼念贴，然后自己不久后也遇难了。然后为他们贴出悼念贴。这种模式一直在持续。

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:24.350
<v Dana Ballout>Something else I saw over and over on the list, journalists-- later in the war-- who'd become aware that they could be making their last reports. They'd say it at the beginning of their videos. And those were the hardest to watch, especially when it was true.
<v Dana Ballout>我在名单上反复看到的另一件事是，记者——在战争后期——意识到他们可能是在做最后的报道。他们在视频开头就会说。这些视频最难看，尤其是当事实确实如此时。

00:42:24.350 --> 00:42:43.490
<v Dana Ballout>One video like that was posted by Ayat Khadoura. Ayat was a freelance journalist and video blogger. Her videos before the war covered a wide range, from what I can tell-- interviews about women in politics. She even appeared in a commercial for ketchup-flavored chips. She clearly liked being in front of the camera.
<v Dana Ballout>阿亚特·卡杜拉（Ayat Khadoura）发布了一段这样的视频。阿亚特是一名自由记者和视频博主。据我所知，她战前的视频涵盖范围很广——关于女性政治的采访。她甚至出演过番茄酱味薯片的广告。她显然喜欢面对镜头。

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:56.410
<v Dana Ballout>Once the war started, Ayat pivoted to covering bombings and food shortages. On November 20, she posted a video report from her home. You can hear the airstrikes hitting very close to where she is. It's scary.
<v Dana Ballout>战争一开始，阿亚特就转向了关于轰炸和食品短缺的报道。11月20日，她从家中发布了一段视频报道。你可以听到空袭在离她很近的地方发生。这太可怕了。

00:42:56.410 --> 00:43:03.860
<v Ayat Khadoura>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Ayat Khadoura>[阿拉伯语演讲]

00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:21.590
<v Dana Ballout>She's saying, this is likely my last video. Today the occupation forces dropped phosphorus bombs on Beit Lehia area and frightening sound bombs. They dropped letters from the sky ordering everyone to evacuate. Everyone ran into the streets in the craziest way. No one knows where to go.
<v Dana Ballout>她说，这很可能是我的最后一段视频。今天占领军在拜特拉希亚（Beit Lehia）地区投下了磷弹和令人恐惧的音爆弹。他们从天空扔下传单，命令所有人撤离。每个人都以最疯狂的方式冲向街头。没人知道该去哪儿。

00:43:21.590 --> 00:43:29.730
<v Ayat Khadoura>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Ayat Khadoura>[阿拉伯语演讲]

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:43.260
<v Dana Ballout>My family and I are split up. Myself and a few others are still at home, but everyone else has evacuated. They don't know where they're going. The situation is so scary. What's happening is so tough, and may God mercy on us.
<v Dana Ballout>我和家人分开了。我和几个人还在家，但其他人都撤离了。他们不知道要去哪儿。局势太可怕了。正在发生的一切太艰难了，愿上帝怜悯我们。

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:51.390
<v Ayat Khadoura>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Ayat Khadoura>[阿拉伯语演讲]

00:43:51.390 --> 00:44:01.360
<v Dana Ballout>And then she begins to cry and the video stops. She was killed later that day.
<v Dana Ballout>然后她开始哭，视频中断。当天晚些时候她被杀害了。

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:28.870
<v Dana Ballout>Targeting journalists, in case you didn't know, is a war crime. So far, the Committee to Protect Journalists has found that three of the journalists on the list were explicitly targeted by the IDF, the Israeli military. Investigations by the Washington Post and Reuters, Human Rights Watch, and the United Nations have also raised serious questions in these three cases. And the Committee to Protect Journalists is investigating 10 other killings.
<v Dana Ballout>顺便说一句，针对记者是战争罪。到目前为止，保护记者委员会发现名单上的三名记者被以色列国防军明确针对。华盛顿邮报、路透社、人权观察和联合国的调查也在这些案件中提出了严重的质疑。保护记者委员会正在调查其他10起死亡案件。

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:45.370
<v Dana Ballout>When we reached out to the IDF for comment, they said, quote, "The IDF has never and will never deliberately target journalists." That's the answer they always give in these situations.
<v Dana Ballout>当我们联系以色列国防军寻求置评时，他们说：“以色列国防军从未也绝不会故意针对记者。”这是他们在这种情况下总是给出的答案。

00:44:45.370 --> 00:45:16.170
<v Dana Ballout>Meanwhile, dozens of seasoned reporters have fled Gaza-- journalists who worked for Al Jazeera, the BBC, the New York Times, the Washington Post, Reuters, Agence France Presse. So many media offices were demolished in Israeli airstrikes that the Committee to Protect Journalists stopped counting. It's not just individual lives that have been destroyed. It's an entire infrastructure.
<v Dana Ballout>与此同时，几十名资深记者逃离了加沙——曾为半岛电视台、BBC、纽约时报、华盛顿邮报、路透社、法新社工作的记者。太多的媒体办公室在以色列空袭中被夷为平地，保护记者委员会已经停止统计了。被摧毁的不仅仅是个体的生命。而是整个基础设施。

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:37.110
<v Dana Ballout>The name on the list that was hardest for me to look at was Issam Abdallah because I'd crossed paths with him once. Issam was a Lebanese journalist, a video journalist for Reuters for many, many years. He had just won an award for coverage of Ukraine. I'm Lebanese and still report there sometimes. And I'd worked with Issam a couple of summers ago.
<v Dana Ballout>名单上最让我难以直视的名字是伊萨姆·阿卜杜拉（Issam Abdallah），因为我曾和他有过一面之缘。伊萨姆是一位黎巴嫩记者，路透社多年的视频记者。他刚刚因为乌克兰的报道获得了一个奖项。我是黎巴嫩人，现在偶尔也会在那里做报道。几年前的夏天我和伊萨姆一起工作过。

00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:54.710
<v Dana Ballout>He helped me film a sort of random story in Beirut. I was interviewing this entrepreneur who had started a sperm-freezing company after an accident where he spilled a tray of hot coffee on his private area, burning himself. I know-- ridiculous. It was a really silly shoot.
<v Dana Ballout>他帮我在贝鲁特拍摄一个随机故事。我当时在采访一位企业家，他在一次事故中把一盘热咖啡洒在私处烧伤后，创办了一家精子冷冻公司。我知道——太荒谬了。那是一次很傻的拍摄。

00:45:54.710 --> 00:46:14.720
<v Dana Ballout>Right after we said cut and started to wrap, Issam started this whole bit about being in his late 30s, reconsidering his own sperm quality, and everything he now realized he was doing to hurt it. And no one could stop laughing. It was a really good day. That felt good to remember-- and to remember him that way.
<v Dana Ballout>在我们说“停”并开始收工后，伊萨姆就开始了整段表演，讲到他在30多岁时重新考虑自己的精子质量，以及他现在意识到自己做的所有伤害它的事情。没人能停止大笑。那真是美好的一天。能记得那一天，并以那种方式记得他，真好。

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:42.660
<v Dana Ballout>Issam was killed by the IDF on October 13. His death was one of the three that the Committee to Protect Journalists has identified as a targeted killing. He was fired upon by an Israeli tank while standing in an empty field on the Lebanon-Israel border with a small group of other journalists. Everyone was wearing press vests with cameras out. They were covering the Hezbollah part of this war. A few other journalists were injured in the attack, which was captured on video.
<v Dana Ballout>伊萨姆于10月13日被以色列国防军杀害。他的死亡是保护记者委员会认定为针对性杀戮的三起案件之一。当他站在黎巴嫩-以色列边境的一片空地上，和一小群其他记者在一起时，遭到了以色列坦克的射击。每个人都穿着媒体马甲，拿着相机。他们当时正在报道这场战争的真主党部分。袭击中还有几名记者受伤，这被视频拍摄了下来。

00:46:42.660 --> 00:47:08.100
<v Dana Ballout>The IDF says they were responding to firing from Hezbollah, not targeting the journalists. But multiple investigations, including by Reuters, the United Nations, Amnesty International, and the AFP found no evidence of any firing from the location of the journalist before the IDF shot at them. The journalists in the group and video footage confirmed that there was no military activity near them.
<v Dana Ballout>以色列国防军称他们是在回应来自真主党的射击，而不是针对记者。但包括路透社、联合国、国际特赦组织和法新社在内的多项调查发现，在以色列国防军向他们开火前，没有任何证据表明该记者所在地有射击。记者群中的人员和视频画面证实，他们附近没有军事活动。

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:33.080
<v Dana Ballout>I'd only met Issam once, barely knew him, but it affected me so much when he died. I know that he understood the risks of his job. But somehow, it still felt so random and unfair that he would be struck down like that, following the rules, wearing his press vest and helmet in a pack of reporters on a sunny day in an open field. I find myself thinking about him all the time.
<v Dana Ballout>我只见过伊萨姆一次，几乎不认识他，但他去世时对我影响巨大。我知道他明白这份工作的风险。但不知为何，他被那样击倒，在阳光明媚的日子里，在开阔地带的一群记者中，遵守规则，穿着媒体马甲戴着头盔，依然感觉那么随机和不公。我发现自己无时无刻不在想他。

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:55.670
<v Dana Ballout>His last Instagram post was commemorating another journalist, this iconic reporter, Shireen Abu Akleh, who'd been killed by the IDF. When I first saw that post in October, I thought, how ironic. Because a week later, Issam also was killed by the IDF. But then after spending time reading the list, I realized how common this had become.
<v Dana Ballout>他最后一条Instagram帖子是在纪念另一位记者，那位标志性的记者希琳·阿布·阿克莱（Shireen Abu Akleh），她之前被以色列国防军杀害。十月份我第一次看到那条帖子时，我想，多讽刺啊。因为一周后，伊萨姆也被以色列国防军杀害了。但在花时间阅读这份名单后，我意识到这种情况已经变得多么普遍。

00:47:55.670 --> 00:48:16.460
<v Dana Ballout>I still haven't finished going through the list and looking up the people on it. I keep finding things that stick with me, like the funny way this one radio host would cut off a caller who was rambling on for too long, a tweet from reporter Ola Atallah that quoted Sylvia Plath. It read, "What ceremony of words can patch the havoc?"
<v Dana Ballout>我还没看完名单并查清上面的人。我不断发现一些让我难忘的东西，比如这个广播主持人切断一个喋喋不休的来电者时的滑稽方式，记者奥拉·阿塔拉（Ola Atallah）引用西尔维娅·普拉斯（Sylvia Plath）的一条推文。它写道：“什么样的词汇仪式能缝补这场浩劫？”

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:38.960
<v Dana Ballout>I'm going to keep going down the list even though this radio story is over now, just for myself, my own way of bearing witness, which is, in the end, all that these journalists were trying to do.
<v Dana Ballout>即便这个广播故事现在结束了，我还是会继续往下看名单，只是为了我自己，我自己的见证方式，而这，最终，也是这些记者试图做的唯一一件事。

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:44.210
<v Ira Glass>Reporter Dana Ballou is journalist and documentary filmmaker. Her story was produced by Diane Wu.
<v Ira Glass>记者达娜·巴鲁特是一名记者和纪录片制作人。她的故事由戴安·吴（Diane Wu）制作。

00:48:44.210 --> 00:49:21.110
<v Ira Glass>["RECORDING 801" BY LINA MAKOUL]
<v Ira Glass>["RECORDING 801" BY LINA MAKOUL]

00:49:21.110 --> 00:49:27.260
<v Ira Glass>This song is by Lina Makoul, a Palestinian musician it's called "Recording 801."
<v Ira Glass>这首歌由巴勒斯坦音乐人莉娜·马库尔（Lina Makoul）演唱，歌名是“录音801”。

00:49:27.260 --> 00:49:43.390
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, A Great Fall. OK, so there are lots of ways to break something. And skateboarding is a pretty good one. David Kestenbaum-- who, of course, you heard earlier in the show-- has been obsessed with this one short video of a skateboarder that he keeps watching over and over.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“重重一摔”。好吧，弄坏东西的方法有很多。滑板就是一种很好的方法。大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆——当然，你之前节目里听到过的——一直痴迷于这一段他反复观看的滑板选手的短视频。

00:49:43.390 --> 00:50:12.670
<v David Kestenbaum>The skateboarder stands at the top of a ramp. The wall of the ramp goes straight down at first, then curves into a flat section, then up again on the other side. It's like an enormous U. You could easily break something from that height. He leans forward. It's 13 and 1/2 feet down. One other number you should know-- 52. That's how old he is in this video-- 52 years old. It's Tony Hawk trying to land a trick that he used to do all the time when he was younger.
<v David Kestenbaum>滑板选手站在坡道顶部。坡道墙壁起初垂直向下，然后弯曲成一个平坦部分，再在另一侧向上。它就像一个巨大的U形。从那个高度你很容易摔坏点什么。他向前倾。那是13.5英尺深。你还应该知道的另一个数字——52。这是他在视频中的年龄——52岁。那是托尼·霍克（Tony Hawk），他试图完成一个年轻时经常做的动作。

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:16.930
<v David Kestenbaum>He goes down the ramp, up the other side, spins in the air and--
<v David Kestenbaum>他滑下坡道，上到另一侧，在空中旋转，然后——

00:50:16.930 --> 00:50:18.610
<v David Kestenbaum>[CRASH]
<v David Kestenbaum>[摔落声]

00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:28.780
<v David Kestenbaum>--falls onto his knees. He's wearing pads, slides to the bottom, then climbs the stairs to try again. But same thing.
<v David Kestenbaum>——摔在膝盖上。他戴着护具，滑到底部，然后爬上楼梯再试。但结果一样。

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:35.440
<v David Kestenbaum>[CRASH]
<v David Kestenbaum>[摔落声]

00:50:35.440 --> 00:50:48.970
<v David Kestenbaum>I was never a skateboard kid-- they were too cool for me. I know Tony Hawk is the most famous skateboarder in the world, but I've only gotten into him now that he's over 50. On social media, he writes these funny little scenes from his life, like this one.
<v David Kestenbaum>我从来不是滑板少年——他们对我来说太酷了。我知道托尼·霍克是世界上最著名的滑板选手，但我是在他50多岁时才开始关注他的。在社交媒体上，他写了生活中一些有趣的片段，比如这个。

00:50:48.970 --> 00:51:15.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Kid at skate park. Are you Tony Hawk? Me-- I am. Him-- no, you're not. OK, I'm not. But are you, for real? I am, for real. I thought you'd look younger. Me too.
<v David Kestenbaum>滑板公园的孩子问：你是托尼·霍克吗？我回答：我是。他：不，你不是。好吧，我不是。但你真的是吗？我：我真的是。他：我以为你会更年轻。我：我也这么想。

00:51:15.400 --> 00:51:49.160
<v David Kestenbaum>So the trick he was trying to do in this video, it's called an Ollie 540. Basically, you go down the ramp, up the other side of the U, then down again-- that a couple times until you get enough height. Then, through some kind of magic, at, like, 15 feet in the air, you spin one and a half times with the board turning underneath you but without grabbing the board. You don't touch it with your hands. Like, you and the board are just rotating it exactly the same speed. And then somehow, you try to land on your skateboard without breaking any bones.
<v David Kestenbaum>他在这段视频里尝试做的动作叫“奥利540”（Ollie 540）。基本上，你滑下坡道，上到U形的另一侧，然后再滑下来——重复几次直到获得足够高度。然后，通过某种魔术，在离地15英尺高的地方，你旋转一圈半，滑板在脚下转动但不能抓板。你不能用手触碰滑板。就像你和滑板以完全相同的速度旋转。然后无论如何，你要尝试落在滑板上而不摔断任何骨头。

00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:50.450
<v David Kestenbaum>[CRASH]
<v David Kestenbaum>[摔落声]

00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:52.020
<v Tony Hawk>Hip.
<v Tony Hawk>“髋部。”

00:51:52.020 --> 00:52:07.860
<v David Kestenbaum>Hip, I think he says there. He's written that the trick has gotten scarier as he's gotten older, just committing to the landing at the end. He wrote, "My willingness to slam unexpectedly into the flat bottom has waned greatly over the last decade."
<v David Kestenbaum>我想他说了“髋部”。他写道，随着年龄增长，这个动作变得更可怕了，仅仅是完成最后的着陆就很困难。他写道：“在过去十年里，我意外猛摔在平地上的意愿大幅减弱了。”

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:27.150
<v David Kestenbaum>One of the things I like about this video is it's basically a collection of failures over and over. It's edited so you can't see how many tries, but it's a lot. He looks tired and just so human. After one fall, he sits there on the ground, looks up, and swears.
<v David Kestenbaum>关于这段视频我喜欢的一点是，它基本上就是一次次失败的集合。经过编辑你看不到尝试了多少次，但非常多。他看起来很累，而且非常人性化。在一次摔倒后，他坐在地上，抬起头，骂了一句。

00:52:27.150 --> 00:52:29.850
<v Tony Hawk>Fuck.
<v Tony Hawk>该死。

00:52:29.850 --> 00:52:36.590
<v David Kestenbaum>The next attempt literally goes sideways. He slides out the end of the ramp and takes out a camera there that's filming.
<v David Kestenbaum>下一次尝试简直侧着去了。他从坡道末端滑出，撞坏了一个正在拍摄的摄像机。

00:52:36.590 --> 00:52:38.070
<v David Kestenbaum>[CRASH]
<v David Kestenbaum>[摔落声]

00:52:38.070 --> 00:52:42.050
<v David Kestenbaum>[CLATTERING]
<v David Kestenbaum>[碰撞声]

00:52:42.050 --> 00:52:48.560
<v Tony Hawk>Sorry. I broke everything.
<v Tony Hawk>抱歉。我把一切都弄坏了。

00:52:48.560 --> 00:53:05.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Shy grin, he checks to see if his finger is OK. Cut to Tony Hawk at the top of the ramp again. Deep breath, skateboard over the void again. Plunges down, up the other side, down, up into the air, around--
<v David Kestenbaum>带着害羞的微笑，他检查手指是否无碍。镜头切回托尼·霍克再次站在坡道顶部。深呼吸，再次滑向虚空。俯冲而下，上到另一侧，下降，冲向空中，旋转——

00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:06.980
<v Tony Hawk>Woo-hoo!
<v Tony Hawk>喔吼！

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:18.720
<v David Kestenbaum>--and he lands it. Lets the board go flying, covers his face with his hands, drops to his knees and says this.
<v David Kestenbaum>——他成功着陆了。滑板飞了出去，他双手捂住脸，跪倒在地，说了这番话。

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:20.610
<v Tony Hawk>I'm kind of sad.
<v Tony Hawk>我有点难过。

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:24.840
<v David Kestenbaum>"I'm kind of sad," he says. One of the film crew fist bumps him.
<v David Kestenbaum>“我有点难过，”他说。摄制组的人员和他碰了碰拳。

00:53:24.840 --> 00:53:29.170
<v Man>Did it! So sick, dude.
<v Man>做到了！太牛了，伙计。

00:53:29.170 --> 00:53:29.890
<v Tony Hawk>[INAUDIBLE].
<v Tony Hawk>[听不清]。

00:53:29.890 --> 00:53:31.750
<v Man>Perfect.
<v Man>完美。

00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:32.680
<v Tony Hawk>I'm a little sad.
<v Tony Hawk>我有点难过。

00:53:32.680 --> 00:53:34.810
<v Tony Hawk>[LAUGHTER]
<v Tony Hawk>[笑声]

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:35.740
<v Man>That was the last one.
<v Man>那是最后一次了。

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:50.310
<v Tony Hawk>Yeah. I never had much finality to anything, but that definitely was the last one I'll ever do. Fuck it. Happy I made it.
<v Tony Hawk>对。我从来没让任何事彻底完结过，但这绝对是我最后一次做这个了。去他的。很高兴我做到了。

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:53.310
<v Man>It was literally perfect.
<v Man>简直完美。

00:53:53.310 --> 00:53:57.000
<v Tony Hawk>Thanks, you guys, for hanging in there with me.
<v Tony Hawk>谢谢你们，一直陪着我。

00:53:57.000 --> 00:54:12.000
<v David Kestenbaum>The title of the video when it got posted was "Tony Hawk lands his last ever Ollie 540, aged 52."
<v David Kestenbaum>视频发布时的标题是“托尼·霍克在52岁时完成了他最后一次奥利540”。

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:42.660
<v David Kestenbaum>I tear up every time I watch this-- in part, because we're basically the same age. He's one year older. Something happens when you're around 50. You can't do things you used to do because stuff hurts. I used to love to run, really love it. I can't anymore-- four knee surgeries. And walking around, I notice things I didn't used to. So many people with little hitches in their steps, working around some bit of pain in a knee, a hip.
<v David Kestenbaum>每次看这个我都流泪——部分是因为我们基本同岁。他比我大一岁。50岁左右的时候，有些事情会发生变化。你不能再做以前能做的事了，因为东西会疼。我以前喜欢跑步，真的非常喜欢。但我现在不能了——做了四次膝盖手术。走在路上，我注意到了以前没注意到的事。这么多人走路一瘸一拐，在膝盖或髋部的某种疼痛中挣扎。

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:55.050
<v David Kestenbaum>I reached out to Tony Hawk back when the video came out. I wanted to talk about it. Apparently, he did not-- declined my interview request. So this is going to be a short story. I'll just say this.
<v David Kestenbaum>视频刚出的时候我就联系过托尼·霍克。我想聊聊这个。显然他没有——拒绝了我的采访请求。所以这将是一个简短的故事。我只想说这个。

00:54:55.050 --> 00:55:44.280
<v David Kestenbaum>A friend told me recently about a doctor who'd gone to work in a small town, ended up treating a lot of older people. And he noticed that the way it would go is that, first, one thing would go wrong. You'd fix it. Then a different thing would go wrong. You'd fix that. Then two things would go wrong at once and then another. Eventually, enough things would break that that's the end. He also said something else. That's the good version, he said. That's the one you want, the one where you get to keep going long enough that all the parts break.
<v David Kestenbaum>一位朋友最近告诉我，有个医生去小镇工作，最终治疗了很多老年人。他注意到情况通常是这样的：首先，一件东西会坏。你修好它。然后另一件不同的东西会坏。你再修好那个。然后两件东西同时坏掉，接着是另一件。最终，足够多的东西损坏了，那就是终点。他还说了另一件事。他说，那是好版本，那是你想要的，那种你能够撑得足够久，久到所有零件都坏掉的版本。

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:54.270
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is still making radio stories at 54. Tony Hawk-- a year after that video-- broke his femur. But after surgeries, he's still skateboarding.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆在54岁时还在制作广播故事。托尼·霍克——那段视频一年后——摔断了股骨。但在手术后，他依然在玩滑板。

00:55:54.270 --> 00:56:23.180
<v Ira Glass>["ALL THE KING'S HORSES" BY SAM GELLAR]
<v Ira Glass>["ALL THE KING'S HORSES" BY SAM GELLAR]

